---
import { massWellPull, massWellReach } from "@void-drift/core";
import { GRAVITY_TUNING } from "@void-drift/mode-a";
import VenusContext from "../../../components/studies/venus/VenusContext.svelte";
import VenusEnvelope from "../../../components/studies/venus/VenusEnvelope.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const venusRadius = 27 * (1 + (12104 / 4879 - 1) / 3);
// Preserve the original comparison; subsequent Practice retuning is recorded below.
const mercuryMass = 1450;
const mercurySurfacePull = massWellPull(
  27,
  mercuryMass,
  GRAVITY_TUNING.falloff,
);
const gravityCandidates = [
  {
    name: "One-third of the surface-gravity difference",
    factor: 1 + (8.87 / 3.7 - 1) / 3,
  },
  { name: "Full surface-gravity ratio", factor: 8.87 / 3.7 },
].map((candidate) => {
  const pull = mercurySurfacePull * candidate.factor;
  const mass = pull / massWellPull(venusRadius, 1, GRAVITY_TUNING.falloff);
  return {
    ...candidate,
    pull,
    mass,
    reach: massWellReach(mass, GRAVITY_TUNING.cutoff, GRAVITY_TUNING.falloff),
  };
});
const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "venus-2026-11-13");
if (!study) throw new Error("Missing Venus study metadata");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2 id="01">01 / A world concealed by cloud</h2>
    <p>
      Practice now gives Mercury a turning mineral surface and Sun-facing light.
      A Venus-like neighbour offers a different material character: the proposed
      starting point is a world concealed by cloud, with a sense of weight
      beneath its envelope. It should belong beside Mercury without borrowing
      its exposed rock texture. The intended change is one reusable Venus-like
      planet drawing demonstrated in the Lab; placing a named Venus into
      Practice is a later decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tension is between a dense, softly layered atmosphere and a clear,
      substantial body at flight scale. Too little structure could leave a blank
      disc; too much contrast or glow could make it read as luminous energy.
      These are visual hypotheses to test. Colour, cloud organisation, edge
      softness, Sun-facing shading and visible cloud motion remain open, judged
      together on one body. A convincing close-up must also survive the smaller
      view beside a ship.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hold the established art direction fixed: compose a world through
      economical forms, convincing material and deliberate light. Use the
      current Practice G7 Sun and implemented Mercury as comparison references,
      without reopening Mercury’s design. Record body radii, lighting and camera
      scale with each example; fixture values do not settle Venus’s size, mass
      or placement. Existing orbital motion, gravity and contact provide
      context. Atmospheric drag, damage, new flight rules and a general planet
      taxonomy are adjacent questions outside this visual cycle.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Question test · 13 November 2026:</strong>
      One subject — Pass. Grounded — Pass. Consequence — Pass. Independent
      review found no corrections needed.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/venus-2026-11-13">Full question test</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="02">02 / Finding a terrestrial atmosphere</h2>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-1">
        02.1 / Opaque cloud texture reads as a gas giant
      </summary>
      <p>
        All three initial treatments read as gas giants in design review.
        Varying veils, bands and billows on an opaque gas surface did not
        establish the intended Venus-like character. Reusing the rock projection
        and lighting was useful; the resulting material language did not
        distinguish this world. This is a finding about these examples, not a
        claim that every opaque atmosphere must read as a gas giant. No variant
        was selected.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          class="evidence"
          src="/studies/venus-2026-11-13/02-1-cloud-comparison.png"
          width="1056"
          height="511"
          alt="The three original cloud sketches, with broad veils, swept bands and broken billows, each beside Mercury."
        >
        <figcaption>
          Original 02.1 capture, 13 November 2026, at a 1280px viewport. Venus
          r30u / Mercury r27u; enlarged 3.2 px/u, small view 0.7 px/u; fixed
          upper-left light (−400, −160), zero Venus spin, deterministic maps.
          Retained to show the shared gas-giant reading; the temporary renderer
          is retired.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-2">02.2 / What the reference is actually showing</summary>
      <p>
        A photograph, a processed scientific image and a game drawing make
        different choices about what to reveal. The next direction separates the
        cloud-covered body from the visual shorthand of conspicuous bands and
        swirls. It does not assume that showing exposed rock is necessary to
        communicate a terrestrial world.
      </p>
      <div class="reference-board">
        <figure>
          <h4>MESSENGER / the quiet globe</h4>
          <img
            src="/studies/venus-2026-11-13/nasa-pia10124.jpg"
            width="320"
            height="320"
            alt="MESSENGER view of Venus as a nearly uniform grey globe against black space."
          >
          <figcaption>
            NASA / Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory /
            Carnegie Institution of Washington. 5 June 2007, PIA10124.
            <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/approaching-venus/"
              >Source and imaging context</a
            >. Broad form dominates the visible image; the surface is hidden by
            cloud. For this study: a quiet disc is a legitimate starting point,
            although it still needs a distinct game character.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <h4>Mariner 10 / processing reveals structure</h4>
          <img
            src="/studies/venus-2026-11-13/nasa-pia23791.jpg"
            width="480"
            height="234"
            alt="Two processed Venus portraits: pale cloud cover on the left, more strongly contrasted cloud structures on the right."
          >
          <figcaption>
            NASA/JPL-Caltech; processing by Kevin M. Gill. 1974 observations,
            2020 processing, PIA23791.
            <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/newly-processed-views-of-venus-from-mariner-10/"
              >Source and method</a
            >. Both are false-colour orange/ultraviolet composites; the right
            increases contrast. These are not two naked-eye appearances. Cloud
            structure is real evidence, but its prominence and colour are
            choices in our drawing.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <h4>Frontier: Elite II / atmosphere at the edge</h4>
          <img
            src="/studies/mercury-2026-11-12/frontier.webp"
            width="320"
            height="200"
            alt="Frontier Earth with broad green and blue regions, a pale atmospheric rim and a nearby ship."
          >
          <figcaption>
            1993; David Braben / Frontier, GameTek / Konami; capture via
            <a href="https://retrorama.fr/en/game/frontier-elite-ii"
              >Retrorama</a
            >. The pale layered limb distinguishes atmosphere from the broad
            surface regions. Borrow that separation of roles, not Earth's
            visible continents or the period rendering style. This is an Earth
            reference, not Venus.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <h4>Escape Velocity / a compact world in flight</h4>
          <img
            src="/studies/mercury-2026-11-12/escape-velocity.webp"
            width="320"
            height="240"
            alt="Escape Velocity flight scene with a small blue-green planet among ships and asteroids."
          >
          <figcaption>
            1996; Matt Burch / Ambrosia Software; capture via
            <a href="https://macemu.com/run/escape-velocity/">Macemu</a>. A
            bounded globe, broad shading and colour carry the planet beside
            ships. Borrow that hierarchy at flight scale; its blue-green
            material is not a Venus palette. Heritage frames are the attributed,
            reduced full-frame references already retained in the Mercury study.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p>
        NASA also reports that Mariner 10's visible-light photographs showed a
        featureless planet while ultraviolet filters revealed cloud detail (<a
          href="https://www.nasa.gov/history/45-years-ago-mariner-10-off-to-venus-and-mercury/"
          >mission history</a
        >). The design inference is to try less internal pattern and give the
        atmospheric edge a separate job. The references do not prove that this
        will solve the gas-giant reading at our scale.
      </p>
      <h4>A new proposal / a quiet ivory envelope</h4>
      <div class="proposal">
        <VenusEnvelope client:load />
        <div>
          <p>
            This sketch replaces the patterned gas surface with a nearly
            continuous pale envelope and a narrow illuminated rim. The body
            keeps its shadow and bounded silhouette; there are no exposed
            continents or craters. It draws on the quiet MESSENGER globe and
            Frontier's separate limb cue.
          </p>
          <p>
            Accepted direction: the quiet ivory body and a distinct illuminated
            atmospheric edge. The restrained shape gives the world a coherent
            character, while the separate edge communicates its envelope. This
            settles the visual approach, not the exact brightness, cloud
            structure or thickness of the atmosphere.
          </p>
          <p>
            Same radius, scales, light direction and Mercury material as 02.1.
            New ivory palette, low-contrast deterministic two-wave veil, zero
            spin; lit arc from 0.92π to 1.64π, width 1.8% of drawn radius
            (minimum 0.65px). The arc is an illustrative cue, not simulated
            scattering. Static material sketch only: no motion, G7 field
            composition or gameplay claim. Reference images remain reference
            material, not textures.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The current example feels too quiet; brighter colour, more structure and
        a larger atmospheric presence remain possibilities for refinement. These
        observations do not select new values or authorize changing this
        reference. The 30u Venus and 27u Mercury radii are comparison fixtures,
        not real Solar System proportions or an accepted size relationship.
        Cloud motion remains untested. The study remains open.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-3">02.3 / Structure within the quiet envelope</summary>
      <p>
        Keep the ivory body and separate lit edge. This comparison asks whether
        broad, faint cloud regions give the envelope enough structure without
        bringing back the belt-and-swirl character of 02.1. The left is the
        unchanged 02.2 reference; the right adds oblique regions with a small
        amount of finer variation. Neither introduces exposed terrain.
      </p>
      <div class="structure-comparison">
        <figure>
          <h4>A / Quiet reference · unchanged</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope client:load />
          <figcaption>
            The accepted direction, with its original faint veil.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <h4>B / Broad cloud regions</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope
            structure={true}
            label="Broad faint cloud regions within the quiet ivory envelope, enlarged and beside Mercury"
            client:load
          />
          <figcaption>
            Structure is the only deliberate variable. Judge the small view as
            well as the enlarged body.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p>
        Both use the same base palette, lighting, 30u body radius, atmospheric
        arc and camera scales as 02.2. The additional deterministic pattern
        varies all three material channels together by up to 14 values out of
        255 before lighting; spin is zero and there is no randomness. This is
        local tonal variation, not a brighter base colour. Mercury remains r27u;
        the relative sizes are still fixtures, not Solar System proportions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Selected: 02.3 B's added cloud structure. Carry its broad, faint regions
        forward within the accepted quiet ivory body and separate atmospheric
        edge. This selects the structure, not a new brightness, size or
        atmospheric thickness. These static sketches do not establish motion or
        production lighting.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-4">02.4 / How much atmosphere at the edge?</summary>
      <p>
        Both examples retain B's material exactly. The left keeps its narrow
        atmospheric edge; the right gives the same illuminated arc more
        thickness. The question is whether that extra presence helps the
        atmosphere read at flight scale while the body keeps its quiet
        character.
      </p>
      <div class="structure-comparison">
        <figure>
          <h4>A / Selected structure · original edge</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope
            structure={true}
            label="Selected 02.3 B structure with original narrow atmosphere"
            client:load
          />
          <figcaption>
            02.3 B unchanged: edge width 1.8% of drawn radius, minimum 0.65px.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <h4>B / A broader atmospheric edge</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope
            structure={true}
            rimWidth={0.065}
            label="Selected cloud structure with broader atmospheric edge"
            client:load
          />
          <figcaption>
            Same arc, colour and opacity; width 6.5% of drawn radius, minimum
            0.65px.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p>
        Fixed: r30u body, r27u Mercury, 3.2 and 0.7 px/u views, zero spin,
        deterministic 02.3 B material and upper-left light. Only arc width
        changes: about 1.73px versus 6.24px enlarged, and 0.65px versus 1.37px
        in the small view. The stroke remains centred on the limb, extending
        equally inward and outward; it does not enlarge the physical body. This
        is an illustrative thickness comparison, not a measured atmosphere or a
        scattering model.
      </p>
      <p>
        Retained finding: the wider atmosphere has better presence in the small,
        zoomed-out view. Enlarged, its hard arc reads as overly simplistic
        vector graphics. The flight-scale presence is preferred; the enlarged
        treatment is not accepted. A later edge investigation must preserve that
        presence while making the atmosphere convincing at both scales.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-5">02.5 / A brighter ivory body</summary>
      <p>
        The current material is pale ivory before shading, but the multiplied
        light mask makes the visible body much darker. This comparison retains
        the selected cloud structure and wider atmospheric edge, and tests a
        lighter shading treatment. The right-hand body lets more of the
        material's brightness through, including on the shadow side. The
        question is whether it gains the pale, cloud-covered character without
        losing its volume.
      </p>
      <div class="structure-comparison">
        <figure>
          <h4>A / Current shaded ivory</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope
            structure={true}
            rimWidth={0.065}
            label="Current ivory body with selected cloud structure and wider edge"
            client:load
          />
          <figcaption>
            Unchanged 02.4 B: full-strength multiplied shading.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <h4>B / More light through the envelope</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope
            structure={true}
            rimWidth={0.065}
            lightStrength={0.55}
            label="Brighter ivory body with the same structure and atmospheric edge"
            client:load
          />
          <figcaption>
            Same material; shading opacity reduced to 55%. The atmosphere and
            Mercury are unchanged.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p>
        Fixed conditions remain r30u Venus, r27u Mercury, 3.2 and 0.7 px/u, zero
        spin, deterministic 02.3 B texture and upper-left illumination. Only
        Venus's multiply-mask opacity changes from 1 to 0.55. This lifts both
        lit and dark regions and reduces shading contrast; it is not a change to
        the material hue, cloud pattern or emitted light. This is a study-only
        tonal proposal, not a physical scattering model or production LFX
        setting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The wider edge's enlarged-view limitation remains deliberately visible.
        This round does not settle it. Judge brightness and the remaining sense
        of mass together, including whether the cloud structure still reads in
        the small view. Finding: the lifted shadows in B lose too much depth,
        and depth is necessary to this planet’s character. Carry A’s stronger
        shading forward. This rules out lifting the whole body this way, not
        exploring richer colours.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-6">
        02.6 / Ivory, amber and orange under the same light
      </summary>
      <p>
        Strong light-to-shadow depth stays fixed. These three treatments colour
        the same cloud material, exploring whether a richer warm palette adds
        character without lifting the shadow. Ivory is the baseline; amber
        brings a golden cloud colour; orange makes a more pronounced,
        deliberately colourized world. These are game-art proposals, not
        measured natural colours.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Mariner 10 pair in 02.2 combines orange and ultraviolet measurements
        into false colour; contrast enhancement changes how prominently its
        cloud structures appear. That supports exploring colour as an authored
        choice, not claiming Venus has one definitive orange appearance.
        <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/venus-from-mariner-10/"
          >NASA’s image-method description</a
        >. The warm alternatives below are our interpretations, not sampled
        reconstructions.
      </p>
      <div class="colour-comparison">
        <figure>
          <h4>A / Ivory reference</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope
            structure={true}
            rimWidth={0.065}
            label="Ivory Venus with strong shading"
            client:load
          />
          <figcaption>
            Base RGB 241, 229, 199. The established quiet material.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <h4>B / Golden amber</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope
            structure={true}
            rimWidth={0.065}
            palette="amber"
            label="Golden amber Venus with the same strong shading"
            client:load
          />
          <figcaption>
            Base RGB 246, 195, 113. A richer golden envelope.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <h4>C / Warm orange</h4>
          <VenusEnvelope
            structure={true}
            rimWidth={0.065}
            palette="orange"
            label="Warm orange Venus with the same strong shading"
            client:load
          />
          <figcaption>
            Base RGB 245, 157, 89. A more saturated colourization.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p>
        Only base material RGB changes. All three retain 02.3 B’s cloud pattern,
        full-strength shading, the 6.5% atmospheric arc, r30u Venus and r27u
        Mercury, zero spin, upper-left light and 3.2 / 0.7 px/u views. The pale
        rim is unchanged to isolate body colour. These palettes are not matched
        for perceived brightness; warmer saturation also changes that
        perception. None emits light, and orange does not encode temperature or
        damage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compare the sense of depth, cloud material and separation from Mercury.
        The open choice is which colour family supports this planet’s character;
        the family retains the range ivory → golden amber → warm orange, with a
        stable colour chosen per planet. Golden amber (02.6 B) is selected for
        the named Venus. Distribution and interpolation of randomized family
        colours remain unspecified; this selection does not animate colour
        during play. The hard atmospheric edge at enlarged scale remains
        unresolved.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-7">02.7 / Return to the whole planet</summary>
      <p>
        The design question concerns the character and readability of one
        cloud-covered world beside Mercury. An enlarged atmospheric edge alone
        cannot settle it. Further edge variations are paused; the unresolved
        close-up treatment does not invalidate the qualities already selected.
      </p>
      <h4>Carry forward</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A quiet cloud-covered body with the broad, faint structure selected in
          02.3 B.
        </li>
        <li>
          Strong light-to-shadow depth. Lifting the whole body in 02.5 removed
          too much of its character.
        </li>
        <li>
          The ivory–amber–orange colour family, stable per planet; golden amber
          for Venus.
        </li>
        <li>
          A distinct atmospheric presence at flight scale. The wider edge helped
          there, without settling its enlarged form.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The hard arc remains provisional. Blending light across the limb
        produced a button-like reading; successive edge corrections did not
        establish a usable replacement. Their drawings are removed rather than
        carried forward as additional design requirements. No new envelope shape
        is selected.
      </p>
      <h4>The next useful evidence</h4>
      <p>
        The existing examples show isolated, static globes under one light
        direction. They have not established how Venus reads beside the actual
        G7 Sun, Mercury and a ship, or through motion and camera changes. The
        proposed next investigation is one composed flight-scale scene using the
        selected body, with the current edge explicitly provisional. It should
        answer whether the whole planet retains its cloud-covered character,
        depth and readable boundary in its intended surroundings before any
        further edge correction is justified.
      </p>
      <p>
        The proposed scene’s fixture size and placement would support
        comparison, not select a Practice orbit or mass. Cloud motion, family
        size range and the final atmospheric treatment remain open. The study
        stays in exploration; this is neither readiness nor implementation
        scope.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-8">
        02.8 / Venus among the Sun, Mercury and a ship
      </summary>
      <p>
        One arrangement, two views. Venus keeps the selected amber material,
        cloud structure and strong directional shading. The wider hard arc is
        provisional and unchanged in form; it is oriented toward the Sun here.
        Inspect the whole world's identity and boundary beside the fitted Wasp,
        Mercury and the Sun's field markings before choosing another detail to
        change.
      </p>
      <VenusContext zoom={0.7} client:load />
      <VenusContext zoom={1.4} client:load />
      <p>
        Static fixture: Practice G7 Sun from seed 71; Mercury r27u at orbital
        distance 258u and angle −0.6 radians; Venus r30u at offset (365, 50)u
        from the Sun; starter Wasp at Venus offset (65, 30)u, heading −π/2.
        Views use 0.7 and 1.4 px/u, with camera centres respectively (170, 0)u
        and (285, 0)u from the Sun. Venus's material phase is zero; time is held
        at zero. This arrangement chooses neither an orbit nor a physical size
        relationship for Venus.
      </p>
      <p>
        The scene uses shared Sun, field and fitted-ship drawing, the Practice
        Mercury material, and production FlightLayers with separate World/LFX
        canvases. Unlike the earlier single-canvas sketches, material and light
        are composed through those layers; any appearance difference is evidence
        to examine, not a new colour decision. Labels are study annotations.
        This tests static composition only: no controls, flying, cloud motion,
        simulation progression or performance claim. Visual inspection shows a
        brighter amber body in this layer composition than in the isolated
        multiply-mask sketches; the selected material values have not changed.
        This is an integration difference to judge, not accepted new brightness.
        Motion remains a later question.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-9">
        02.9 / One-third of the relative size difference
      </summary>
      <p>
        Deliberate extension of the visual inquiry: apply Venus-to-Mercury size
        and mean orbital-distance ratios to the existing Practice Mercury,
        reduce the size difference to one-third, and inspect the result with
        shared world HUD. Venus keeps golden amber, the selected cloud
        structure, strong lighting and the provisional wide edge.
      </p>
      <VenusContext zoom={0.4} relativeHud={true} client:load />
      <VenusContext zoom={0.7} relativeHud={true} client:load />
      <p>
        Using NASA's diameters 12,104km / 4,879km and mean Sun distances 108.2 /
        57.9 million km: the full size ratio gave Venus radius 66.98u. The
        current proposal uses one-third of its difference from Mercury: 27 +
        (66.98 − 27) / 3 =
        <strong>40.33u</strong>, about 1.49 times Mercury’s radius. The
        centre-to-centre orbit remains 258 × 108.2 / 57.9 =
        <strong>482.13u</strong>, or 1.87 times Mercury’s orbital distance.
        <a
          href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/index.html?authuser=0"
          >NASA planetary fact sheet</a
        >. Size and distance retain Practice's separate compressions; the Sun
        remains its current 70u radius. This is not one uniformly scaled Solar
        System.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shared HUD primitives draw both orbits, outward name leaders and
        Mercury's existing gravity boundary. Venus has no gravity ring because
        its mass and gravity tuning are not selected; appearance and orbital
        distance do not determine that gameplay value. The Sun retains its field
        markings. Venus's nearest surface is about 441.81u from the Sun's
        centre, outside Practice's roughly 324u charging reach.
      </p>
      <p>
        Static Venus angle 0.14 radians; Mercury −0.6. The Wasp sits 35u beyond
        Venus's radius horizontally and 30u below its centre. Overview camera:
        Sun-centred, 0.4 px/u; closer camera: 190u right of the Sun, 0.7 px/u.
        This preserves the earlier 02.8 fixture separately. Selected for Venus:
        radius 40.33u and centre-to-centre orbit 482.13u, using the exact ratio
        formulas above. This settles size and orbital distance, not orbital
        speed, cloud rotation, mass or gravity reach. The atmosphere and
        brighter layered appearance remain unresolved. The study stays in
        exploration.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-10">02.10 / Cloud rotation under fixed light</summary>
      <p>
        Venus holds its selected 40.33u radius and 482.13u orbital distance.
        Only the cloud map turns: light, atmospheric edge, ship, Mercury and
        camera remain fixed. Compare a 30-second turn with a quieter 60-second
        turn. Watch whether features travel around a sphere while the shadow
        stays anchored to the Sun, and whether the pace suits this world's
        character.
      </p>
      <h4>A / One turn in 30 seconds</h4>
      <video
        muted
        controls
        preload="metadata"
        width="1000"
        height="480"
        src="/studies/venus-2026-11-13/turn-30.webm"
        aria-label="Venus clouds turning once every 30 seconds"
      ></video>
      <h4>B / One turn in 60 seconds</h4>
      <video
        muted
        controls
        preload="metadata"
        width="1000"
        height="480"
        src="/studies/venus-2026-11-13/turn-60.webm"
        aria-label="Venus clouds turning once every 60 seconds"
      ></video>

      <p>
        Recorded visual comparison, 20 frames per second, 60 seconds per clip.
        Both recordings begin at cloud-clock 0.5 seconds; phase is clock × 2π
        divided by the respective turn period. No random seed is used for the
        cloud map. Same deterministic golden-amber 02.3 B map, shared spherical
        projection, World/LFX composition and 02.9 close camera at 0.7 px/u.
        Increasing longitude phase makes the visible features travel right to
        left; this is a visual direction fixture, not an accepted astronomical
        spin convention. Cloud turn is independent of orbit.
        <strong>Selected: 02.10 B, one cloud-map turn every 60 seconds.</strong>
        Retain the demonstrated right-to-left motion under fixed Sun-facing
        light. This selects visible cloud rotation, not orbital speed or a
        physical rotation period for the hidden solid surface.
      </p>
      <p>
        This isolates rigid cloud-map rotation, not evolving weather. Mercury's
        material and the Sun remain frozen; no flight simulation advances. The
        Motion instrument currently provides ship-path measures and a 14-second
        cursor, not a planet-material turn comparison. These recordings avoid
        changing that instrument's contract. A configurable planet visual track
        with a longer shared timeline would be a separate instrument extension
        to discuss if interactive investigation becomes necessary.
      </p>
    </details>
    <h3 id="02-11">02.11 / Orbital speed from the relative numbers</h3>
    <p>
      Anchor Venus's travel speed to Practice Mercury's 4u/s. NASA's rounded
      mean orbital velocities are 35.0km/s for Venus and 47.4km/s for Mercury,
      giving a ratio of about 0.7384. Apply that ratio to linear travel along
      the orbit, then derive angular speed from the selected orbital radius.
      <a href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/"
        >NASA planetary fact sheet</a
      >.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Treatment</th>
          <th>Venus travel speed</th>
          <th>Full orbit at 482.13u</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Full relative difference</td>
          <td>2.95u/s</td>
          <td>17.1 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>One-third of the difference</td>
          <td>3.65u/s</td>
          <td>13.8 minutes</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Full ratio: 4 × 35 / 47.4 = 2.9535865u/s. One-third alternative: 4 × [1 +
      (35 / 47.4 − 1) / 3] = 3.6511955u/s. Orbit periods use circumference
      divided by travel speed. Mercury's 258u orbit at 4u/s takes about 6.8
      minutes. Venus's selected 60-second cloud turn is independent.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong
        >Selected: full relative difference, approximately 2.95u/s.</strong
      >
      The numerical comparison is sufficient for this decision: the full-ratio
      pace is not too fast by the numbers, so compressing the difference is
      unnecessary. Retain the exact ratio formula; angular speed is travel speed
      divided by the selected 482.13u orbital radius, using its exact formula
      from 02.9. This is a design decision based on speeds and periods, not a
      claim of observed motion quality. No new animation was required or used to
      reach it. HUD tracking and Sun-facing light through orbital movement
      remain implementation checks.
    </p>
    <h3 id="02-12">02.12 / What should the larger world's gravity mean?</h3>
    <p>
      This round compares gravity references for the selected size and orbit. A
      larger visible body does not imply a larger gravity field. The proposed
      comparison holds the game's inverse-square law and cut-off fixed and
      varies the pull at the body's surface relative to Mercury. This gives the
      larger world a distinct pull without assuming its compressed visual size
      implies a real planetary mass. It is an extension into encounter tuning;
      the selected appearance and motion remain fixed.
    </p>
    <p>
      NASA lists mean surface gravity as 8.87m/s² for Venus and 3.70m/s² for
      Mercury: a ratio of about 2.40. As with size, we can retain the full
      difference or one-third of it. Here those factors multiply Mercury's game
      surface pull; they do not put SI acceleration directly into the game.
      Sources:
      <a href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/venusfact.html"
        >Venus</a
      >
      and
      <a
        href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/mercuryfact.html?level=1"
        >Mercury</a
      >.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Reference / proposal</th>
          <th>Surface pull relative to Mercury</th>
          <th>Game mass</th>
          <th>Gravity reach from centre</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Practice Mercury · r27u</td>
          <td>1.00×</td>
          <td>{mercuryMass.toFixed(0)}</td>
          <td>
            {massWellReach(
              mercuryMass,
              GRAVITY_TUNING.cutoff,
              GRAVITY_TUNING.falloff,
            ).toFixed(1)}u
          </td>
        </tr>
        {gravityCandidates.map((candidate) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{candidate.name} · Venus r40.33u</td>
            <td>{candidate.factor.toFixed(2)}×</td>
            <td>{candidate.mass.toFixed(0)}</td>
            <td>{candidate.reach.toFixed(1)}u</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      For the current inverse-square law, mass is Mercury's mass × (Venus radius
      / Mercury radius)² × the chosen surface-pull factor. The shared mass law
      calculates reach from that mass and the unchanged 50u/s² cut-off. Radius
      remains 40.33u and orbital distance 482.13u. Surface pull is a reference
      calculation, not the pull at ship contact: a ship's centre stops one
      collision radius farther out, where pull is lower.
    </p>
    {gravityCandidates.map((candidate, index) => (
      <>
        <h4>
          {index === 0 ? "A" : "B"} / {candidate.name}
        </h4>
        <VenusContext
          zoom={0.4}
          relativeHud={true}
          venusMass={candidate.mass}
          client:load
        />
      </>
    ))}
    <p>
      These are the same Sun-centred 0.4 px/u scene with a proposed Venus
      gravity boundary added. A shows a more restrained reach; B extends the
      region in which Venus can contribute pull. Boundaries may overlap the Sun
      and Mercury: the existing game adds those accelerations as vectors.
      Crossing a line is a cut-off in that model, not a new atmospheric effect.
      Practice tuning has not been changed. Mercury's current field is a
      comparison baseline, not a newly endorsed final value. A smaller Mercury
      field and a Venus field larger than Mercury's current one remain an
      untested possibility; this round does not retune either planet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Selected: A, one-third of the surface-gravity difference, for Practice.
      Its resulting field works well in this composition. This accepts the
      resulting tuning value, while retaining the reservation about coupling
      surface size and gravity; it does not establish a general rule that larger
      bodies need larger fields. Keep the exact mass calculation above for this
      Venus stamp. Mercury remains unchanged.
    </p>
    <p>
      The diagrams establish geometry, not slingshot quality or escape
      difficulty. Those remain integration checks. No further gravity comparison
      is needed to preserve this decision. The atmospheric edge at enlarged
      scale and the brightness difference between isolated and composed
      rendering remain provisional; neither calls for another correction loop
      here.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="03">03 / Ready for a Practice implementation</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Delivery · 13 November 2026:</strong>
      this reference was requested in Practice. The named amber Venus now
      replaces Practice's middle gas planet, retaining Mercury and the outer
      rock. The shared material, orbit, mass, cloud turn, directional light and
      World HUD can be inspected in the
      <a href="/lab/entities/planet/practice"
        >production Practice planet preview</a
      >. The selections below remain the design reference; implementation checks
      do not settle encounter feel or the provisional enlarged atmospheric arc.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exploration supplies a coherent Venus stamp for an alpha trial: golden
      amber, broad faint cloud structure from 02.3 B, strong shaded depth, the
      provisional wider atmospheric arc, and the accepted size, orbit, cloud
      turn, travel speed and gravity below. No further appearance variants are
      needed before implementation. This is feasibility for delivery; the
      composed diagrams do not establish encounter feel.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Practice retuning · 13 November 2026:</strong>
      after integration, Mercury was requested at 3/5 of its previous mass: 870.
      Reusing selected formula A gives Venus mass 2844.850581, stored
      independently. The resulting reaches are 131.91u for Mercury and 238.53u
      for Venus at the unchanged 50u/s² cut-off. Both pulls are 60% of their
      initial values; size, orbit and motion are unchanged. The comparisons and
      accepted table below retain the original 1450 baseline as the design
      history.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Property</th>
          <th>Accepted reference</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Material</td>
          <td>
            02.3 B structure; 02.6 B golden amber, RGB (246, 195, 113); retain
            depth
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Radius</td>
          <td>27 × (1 + (12104 / 4879 − 1) / 3) ≈ 40.33u</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Orbital distance</td>
          <td>258 × 108.2 / 57.9 ≈ 482.13u, centre to centre</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cloud turn</td>
          <td>
            02.10 B: 60 seconds, demonstrated right-to-left; independent of
            orbit and light
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Travel speed</td>
          <td>
            4 × 35 / 47.4 ≈ 2.954u/s; angular speed = travel speed / orbital
            distance
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Gravity mass</td>
          <td>
            1450 × (accepted radius / 27)² × (1 + (8.87 / 3.70 − 1) / 3) ≈
            {gravityCandidates[0].mass.toFixed(1)}
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Gravity reach</td>
          <td>
            {gravityCandidates[0].reach.toFixed(1)}u at inverse-square falloff
            and cut-off 50u/s²
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The mass formula documents this selection against Mercury's present 1450
      baseline. Implementation should store the selected Venus mass
      independently: later changes to Mercury or visible radius must not
      silently retune Venus. This is an accepted Practice value, not a universal
      surface-gravity rule.
    </p>
    <h3>Reuse the established rendering and simulation</h3>
    <p>
      The study already composes SphericalSurface, RockBodyPainter,
      FlightLayers, the production Sun, fitted Wasp and shared world HUD.
      Promote the deterministic cloud atlas and envelope drawing into a reusable
      planet material; keep the body on World and directional lighting on LFX.
      FlightDeck currently loads a Mercury-specific asset and flight-frame
      dispatches Mercury-specific body, light and HUD passes. Extend that path
      to a second named material rather than putting a study component into the
      game.
    </p>
    <p>
      Simulation already advances circular orbits, sums mass-well accelerations
      and handles planet contact. Practice currently customizes the stock moon
      as Mercury; Venus needs an explicit Practice identity and independently
      configured mass. The stock planet factory is also used by other modes.
      Decide the Practice roster mapping during implementation and check every
      retained body's orbit separation, spawn clearance and gravity overlap. A
      new Practice body must receive a mass before gravity tuning is applied. No
      new gravity law, atmospheric collision rule or flight control is required.
    </p>
    <h3>Limits carried into delivery</h3>
    <p>
      The wide arc reads better at small scale but remains simplistic enlarged;
      soft blending produced a button-like result. Preserve the provisional arc
      for the first integration. The composed World/LFX scene is brighter than
      the isolated multiply-mask example: use the accepted composed view as the
      integration reference while retaining visible cloud depth. Do not reopen
      these as an automatic sequence of corrections.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ivory–amber–orange family is accepted, but its random distribution and
      interpolation are unspecified. A fixed amber Venus can ship independently;
      procedural family spawning is deferred. Orbit phase is a fixture
      condition, not an accepted placement rule. Moving HUD alignment,
      Sun-facing light, overlap of gravity fields and contact behavior require
      implementation checks. The existing gravity contact-pull guardrail must be
      evaluated for this body; any mismatch needs an explicit Practice-specific
      contract decision, not a silent change to the selected mass or a weakened
      global test.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="04">04 / Deliver one named Venus, then the family</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Design-principle changes: none proposed.</strong>
      The existing natural-body art direction supports this material. Feature
      contracts need updating; the planet spec's older flat-disc descriptions do
      not describe the accepted Mercury and Venus presentation accurately.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Reusable cloud material — medium.</strong>
        Extract the atlas, projection, envelope and palette inputs from the
        study into shared asset code. Preserve 02.3 B structure, 02.6 B colour,
        02.10 B rotation and directional light. Demonstrate through the Lab
        planet surface using the shared renderer. Check flight-scale and
        enlarged rendering, World/LFX composition, cached generation and frame
        cost. No per-frame atlas noise.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Practice Venus definition — medium; independent of material
          work.</strong
        >
        Define the exact size, distance, speed, spin and fixed selected mass in
        mode-a. Define or extend Zod contracts first if identity or
        configuration shapes change. Apply only to Practice, preserving Mercury
        and other modes. Update the planets and gravity-field feature specs.
        Verify deterministic orbit updates, mode isolation, valid
        roster/placement, summed gravity, cut-off and contact with the existing
        simulation tests where appropriate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Practice rendering and HUD — medium; depends on both slices.</strong
        >
        Wire the material through FlightDeck and flight-frame. Reuse world
        labels, orbit paths and mass-derived gravity boundaries. Update the
        render spec and relevant frame contracts. Check that body, light and HUD
        follow the same moving entity, cloud rotation stays independent, pause
        freezes game time consistently, and material loading has a safe
        fallback. Inspect the accepted composition at 0.4 and 0.7 px/u and the
        enlarged view; verify keyboard and landscape touch play remain connected
        to the game loop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Family rollout — deferred, separately sized.</strong>
        Keep the ivory–gold–orange range as the accepted direction. Choose
        deterministic sampling and eligible worlds only when procedural family
        spawning is requested. This does not block named Venus in Practice.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Slices 1 and 2 can be developed independently; slice 3 delivers the
      composed Practice result. Required checks belong to their changed systems,
      with automated correctness separate from visual fidelity and gameplay
      judgment. This scope does not start implementation or claim that flyby and
      escape behavior have been demonstrated.
    </p>
    <p>
      The study is at scope review. The existing visual examples remain
      available to compare the implementation against the selected material and
      composition. Before final archival closure, retain useful static captures
      alongside the existing rotation clips and retire temporary apparatus
      without continuing consumers. The accepted decisions above stand
      independently of those tools.
    </p>
  </section>
